Part II — Verify or falsify our existing hypotheses

Prior research produced the following hypotheses.

Do not accept them automatically.

Test every one against the current source.

Classify each:

CONFIRMED;

PARTIALLY TRUE;

STALE;

FALSE;

UNKNOWN / NEEDS DEVICE TEST.

H1 — Vulkan setting contract

There may be a "useVulkan" setting exposed at the Kotlin/UI configuration level that is not fully persisted, does not necessarily trigger reload, and may not actually be passed into native model initialization.

Determine the exact current behavior.

H2 — GPU layer reporting

Prism may currently infer actual GPU layer placement from something approximately equivalent to:

"gpu_layers_requested > 0 && llama_supports_gpu_offload()"

and then report the requested count as if that were the verified count.

Determine whether Prism really knows:

how many layers were actually offloaded;

which device owns them;

CPU model-buffer bytes;

GPU model-buffer bytes;

KV allocation location;

compute-buffer allocation.

H3 — Prefill and decode threads

Prism may currently use one thread-count value for both:

autoregressive token generation; and

prompt/batch processing.

Determine whether separate:

decode threads;

batch/prefill threads

would expose useful optimization opportunities.

H4 — "n_batch" and "n_ubatch"

Prism may currently configure physical microbatch size equal to logical batch size.

Investigate whether independently tuning:

"n_batch";

"n_ubatch"

could improve Android memory use, prompt throughput, cancellation responsiveness, or accelerator behavior.

H5 — Model-fit memory estimation

Prism's readiness system may use a simplified KV estimate rather than architecture-specific KV calculations.

Determine what metadata Prism already extracts from GGUF.

Design a better estimate based on applicable factors such as:

layers;

KV heads;

head dimensions;

context;

K cache representation;

V cache representation;

GQA/MQA;

sliding-window attention;

model architecture;

compute buffers;

backend placement.

Do not blindly use an equation that is invalid for some architectures.

H6 — Long prompt truncation

The native engine may truncate oversized prompts by removing tokens from the beginning.

Determine whether this can discard:

system instructions;

tool schema;

high-priority context.

Compare token-level truncation against message-aware context construction.

H7 — Prefix cache reuse

Prism appears to reuse common prompt-prefix KV state.

Determine:

whether the algorithm is correct;

when it invalidates;

what happens after context shifting;

how much it saves in realistic multi-turn chat;

whether upstream llama.cpp now offers safer/better primitives.

H8 — Context shifting

Prism appears to contain custom KV compaction/sliding-window logic.

Attack it for:

positional correctness;

system-prefix detection failures;

architecture incompatibility;

RoPE issues;

models with native sliding-window attention;

tool/system prompt corruption;

malformed active-token accounting.

Determine whether it should remain custom, move to upstream primitives, or become model-aware.

H9 — Model loading

Prism may currently:

attempt mmap first;

fall back to non-mmap;

keep "mlock" disabled;

enable tensor checking;

lack explicit weight-repacking policy.

Benchmark/research whether these should be:

fixed defaults;

per-device choices;

per-model choices;

import-time validation choices;

auto-tuned policies.

H10 — Vulkan compile features

Some Vulkan features such as:

integer dot products;

cooperative matrices;

cooperative matrix v2

may currently be disabled for compatibility.

Research whether modern Android hardware can benefit from selectively enabling them.

Do not enable them globally without evidence.

H11 — OpenCL

Prism has or had an experimental Adreno OpenCL path.

Determine:

current implementation state;

supported quantization/model restrictions;

performance;

reliability;

maintenance cost;

whether it should become a production backend.

H12 — KleidiAI

Determine exactly what KleidiAI acceleration Prism currently receives.

Test whether:

thread affinity;

performance-core selection;

thread counts;

quant type;

model size

materially alter results.

H13 — Speculative decoding

Prism may compile llama.cpp speculative/N-gram code without integrating it into the live generation path.

Investigate:

draft-model speculation;

N-gram-simple;

N-gram-map/mod;

EAGLE or other upstream modes where applicable.

Prioritize approaches suitable for phones.

Measure memory and thermal cost, not just tok/s.

Part III — Competing engine research

Perform a deep technical investigation of each engine/project below.

Use current source code, documentation, recent releases, issues, PRs, benchmark methodology, and implementation details.

Mandatory targets

A. llama.cpp upstream

Repository:
"ggml-org/llama.cpp"

This is Prism's base runtime and must receive its own adversarial comparison against Prism's integration.

Investigate all relevant recent Android/mobile improvements Prism has not adopted.

B. PocketPal AI

Repository:
"a-ghorbani/pocketpal-ai"

C. llama.rn

Repository:
"mybigday/llama.rn"

Treat PocketPal and llama.rn separately where appropriate:

PocketPal = product/runtime policy;

llama.rn = native bridge/runtime substrate.

D. MNN / MNN LLM

Repository:
"alibaba/MNN"

E. MLC LLM

Repository:
"mlc-ai/mlc-llm"

F. ExecuTorch

Repository:
"pytorch/executorch"

G. LiteRT-LM / Google AI Edge LLM runtime

Investigate the current relevant open-source repository/project.

H. ChatterUI

Investigate the current repository and its mobile local inference implementation.

I. SmolChat or its current successor/project

Verify whether it remains relevant.

If it is inactive, record that.

Part IV — Find competitors and optimization approaches we missed

Do not limit research to the projects listed above.

Search GitHub and current technical literature for additional serious OSS mobile/on-device inference systems.

Potential categories include:

Qualcomm QNN/Hexagon;

MediaTek NPU;

Samsung accelerator paths;

ONNX Runtime GenAI;

TensorFlow Lite/LiteRT;

