Part XV — Agent execution missions

This is one of the most important deliverables.

Transform the roadmap into self-contained agent mission packets.

Another orchestration agent should be able to dispatch these directly.

Each mission must contain:

Mission ID

Example:

"PIR-03"

Objective

One precise outcome.

Why it matters

Research/evidence basis.

Starting commit

Use current main or dependency branch.

Allowed scope

Exact files/directories.

Files likely to modify

List them.

Files the agent should avoid

Prevent collisions.

Dependencies

Mission IDs that must finish first.

Implementation steps

Concrete steps.

Required tests

Exact or likely commands based on repository tooling.

Benchmark requirements

If performance-related.

Acceptance criteria

Objective evidence.

Failure/stop conditions

When not to proceed.

Deliverables

Code, report, benchmark CSV, documentation, etc.

Evidence to record

Configuration and measured results.

Parallel execution strategy

Design missions so multiple agents can work concurrently without unnecessary conflicts.

Identify:

which missions can run in parallel;

which require isolated Git worktrees;

which modify shared central files;

which should be owned by the integrator;

merge order.

Where multiple missions would modify the same central files, prefer:

leaf experimentation modules first;

integrator-owned wiring later.

Do not assign five parallel agents to edit "Engine.cpp" simultaneously.

Use isolated branches/worktrees for experimental backends.

Root integrator mission

Create a final dedicated mission for the lead integration agent.

Responsibilities should include:

collect completed mission branches;

verify benchmark artifacts;

reject unsupported optimizations;

cherry-pick/merge in dependency order;

resolve shared-file integration;

run full unit/instrumentation/native build gates;

rerun the benchmark baseline;

compare before/after;

update architecture documentation;

produce final performance/correctness report.

The integrator must not blindly merge every experiment.

Part XVI — Prioritization framework

